# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the test run (see TSAN_OPTIONS in the tsan test preset).
#
# trantor (Drogon's network library, v1.5.28) shutdown race: Drogon's own main-loop thread runs
# EventLoop::quit() on each IO loop (wakeup through its eventfd, read of the loop's thread id) while
# that loop's thread, which already saw the quit flag, exits and destroys its EventLoop in a
# thread-local destructor (close of the eventfd, free of the object). The test harness cannot order
# this: quit() is issued by Drogon's thread, not ours, and queueing it on the loop is the same call.
# It is seen once per Drogon test executable, at the very end of the run, in some runs only.
#
# Caveat: TSan matches a `race:` entry when ANY frame of either stack matches. These two entries
# can therefore also hide a race on one of this project's objects that is destroyed or touched
# during that same loop teardown (for example a captured lambda in a queued functor). Only the
# shutdown race above has been observed. To re-check, remove the entries (or the suppressions=
# option in CMakePresets.json) and run `scripts/dev-tsan ctest --preset tsan` a few times: any
# report other than the EventLoop::quit / ~EventLoop pair is a real finding.
race:trantor::EventLoop::~EventLoop
race:trantor::EventLoop::quit
